{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2114"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2114"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1444735053#5_3253209175","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2114","rank":4,"snippet":"December 6, 2018 John Kollman, MS 1; Holly L. Sobotka, MS 1 ( View author affiliations) PEER REVIEWED Abstract Introduction Methods Results Conclusion Introduction Methods Data sources Statistical analyses Results Cancer incidence<cover>Cancer mortality Stage at diagnosis Cancer risk factors Health insurance status Treatment status Discussion Acknowledgments Author Information References Tables View Page In: pdf icon \" , \"segment\": \"One study using US data found that rates for some cancers differed by as much as a factor of 2 between the poorest groups and the most affluent groups (3). Residents of poor areas are also more likely than residents of wealthier areas to receive a diagnosis cancer at a late stage (4). We examined cancer disparities in Ohio to determine whether the relationship between poverty and cancer was apparent at the county level. Ohio is suitable for such an analysis because the state’s poverty rates vary by county, ranging from 4.5% to 33.0%, such that poor counties are mostly in the Appalachian region </cover>of Ohio and the more affluent counties are mostly suburban and do not include metropolitan areas. The objective of this study was to describe differences in cancer incidence and mortality rates, ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1444735053#5_3253209175","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":16},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":19,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2114"},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":19,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2114"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2114"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1444735053#6_3253211711","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2114","rank":5,"snippet":"Results Conclusion Introduction Methods Data sources Statistical analyses Results Cancer incidence<cover>Cancer mortality Stage at diagnosis Cancer risk factors Health insurance status Treatment status Discussion Acknowledgments Author Information References Tables View Page In: pdf icon \" , \"segment\": \"Top Methods We studied populations in the 12 poorest and the 10 most affluent counties in Ohio. These counties were identified by the percentage of the county population living in poverty in the 2011–2015 American Community Survey (6). The US Census Bureau uses a set of income thresholds that vary by family size and composition to determine who is living in poverty. In 2015, for example, the minimum family income threshold for poverty was $12,331 for a person younger than 65 and $24,036 for a family of 4 (2 adults and 2 children aged <18 y) (7). Twelve counties in Ohio had poverty rates of 20% or more in 2011–2015 (Adams, Ashtabula, Athens, Gallia, Highland, Jackson, Lucas, Meigs, Morgan, Pike, Scioto, and Vinton) and were defined as the poorest counties (8). All 12 counties, except Lucas County, are in the Appalachian region. </cover>The population in this group of counties was 904,834 in 2010, ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1444735053#5_3253209175","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":19,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
