{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"kentucky\" \"cancer mortality\" (+ highest high) (+ smoking tobacco poverty screening prevention))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"kentucky\" \"cancer mortality\" (+ highest high) (+ smoking tobacco poverty screening prevention))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#2_1837064550","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2118","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#2_1837064550\" , \"end_char\": 2330.000000 , \"headings\": \"U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates Cancer in the US US States with Highest Cancer Mortality Rates Cancer Health Disparities The Burden of <cover>Poverty U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates \" , \"segment\": \"it is presented as the number of deaths per 100,000 population. In the US, the three states with the highest cancer mortality rates are Kentucky (</cover>199.3 deaths out of 100,000 cancer patients), Mississippi (196.5), and West Virginia (190.5). These also happen to be the states that are consistently named the poorest in the country, both in economy and health. They are included in the southern region (with the exception of West Virginia which is sometimes southern, sometimes eastern) which some researchers have deemed the “cancer belt” of the US. Other southern states with high mortality rates include Arkansas (189.6), Louisiana (188.7), Oklahoma (185.4), Tennessee (185.4), and Alabama (182.","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#2_1837064550","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":43},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#4_1837068385","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2118","rank":2,"snippet":", \"end_char\": 3743.000000 , \"headings\": \"U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates Cancer in the US US States with Highest Cancer Mortality Rates Cancer Health Disparities The Burden of Poverty U.S. States With The <cover>Highest Cancer Mortality Rates \" , \"segment\": \"A person’s socioeconomic status negatively affects their access to a higher quality of life, decreases their chance of having adequate health insurance, and often exposes them to dangerous jobs and housing locations with increased exposure to environmental toxins. It also influences controllable cancer risk factors resulting in a higher probability of smoking, drinking, sedentary activity, and obesity. Low socioeconomic status disproportionately affects African Americans, Latinos, and Whites living in the Appalachian region (Kentucky, </cover>West Virginia, and Tennessee from the list). These individuals are also more likely to die from cancer. The Burden of Poverty But with such advanced medical technology in the US, why do so many people fall victim to cancer? All of the states on this list suffer from a lack of resources to carry out public health outreach ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#4_1837068385","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":5},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#3_1837066437","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2118","rank":3,"snippet":"states are due to a number of factors, namely, low socioeconomic status. Residents of these states are likely to have a low socioeconomic status meaning they have attained lower levels of education, income, and community status. A person’s socioeconomic status negatively affects their access to a higher quality of life, decreases their chance of having adequate health insurance, and often exposes them to dangerous jobs and housing locations with increased exposure to environmental toxins. It also influences controllable cancer risk factors resulting in a higher probability of smoking, drinking, sedentary activity, and obesity. Low socioeconomic status disproportionately affects African Americans, Latinos, and Whites living in the Appalachian region (<cover>Kentucky, West Virginia, and Tennessee from the list). These individuals are also more likely to die from cancer. The Burden of Poverty But with such advanced medical technology in the US, why do so many people fall victim to cancer?\" , \"start_char\": 1733.000000 , \"title\": \"U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality </cover>Rates - WorldAtlas\" , \"url\": \"https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/u-s-states-with-the-highest-cancer-mortality-rates.html\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#3_1837066437","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":4},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213817","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2118"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#5_1837070382","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2118","rank":4,"snippet":"suffer from a lack of resources to carry out public health outreach campaigns like education and screenings. Many cancers, if diagnosed early on, can be prevented or successfully treated. When the state cannot invest in public health and when the people themselves cannot afford access to healthcare, health screening and prevention are often overlooked. These areas, therefore, have a reactive rather than a proactive approach to healthcare which can have fatal consequences when dealing with a cancer diagnosis. Indeed, one could say that <cover>poverty is actually the root cause of high mortality rates in these states. U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates Rank State Cancer Deaths Per 100,000 People Per Year 1 Kentucky</cover>199.3 2 Mississippi 196.5 3 West Virginia 190.5 4 Arkansas 189.6 5 Louisiana 188.7 6 Oklahoma 185.4 7 Tennessee 185.4 8 Alabama 182.1 9 Indiana 179.4 10 Missouri 179.1 Amber Pariona April 25 2017 in Society Home Society U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates\" , \"start_char\": 3096.000000 , \"title\": \"U.S. States With The Highest Cancer Mortality Rates - ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#2_1837064550","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#4_1837068385","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_1248095210#3_1837066437","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":4},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
